speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, the question which is under discussion is the motion which has been, moved by the hon. member for Athabaska (Mr. Kellner). It reads as follows:
Whereas the need for establishment of national highways is greater than in 1919;

National Highways-Mr. McMillan
And whereas in that year the Dominion government recognized its responsibility in this matter by a substantial grant, which grant is now exhausted;
Therefore be it resolved that in the opinion of this house the government should consider the advisability that a further federal grant be made to cover the next five years' development of highway construction.
And the hon. member for Kindersley (Mr. Carmichael) has moved an amendment thereto, to provide for a national highway across Canada at the earliest possible date.
I am amused, if not surprised, by the tactics of our hon. friends across the way. That men of such commanding ability, many of *whom are from the province of Ontario, should think they could put this over the members on this side is something that I can scarcely credit, for I have a higher opinion of their good judgment. I may say that I have a very high regard for the hon. loader of the opposition (Mr. Bennett). I admire his courage and his wonderful political fighting qualities, but I am surprised that he should tackle such a subject at this time. Possibly one of the most difficult tasks in the political life of Canada has fallen to the lot of my hon. friend. To criticize the policies of a government when national affairs are not satisfactory is a comparatively easy matter; but, sir, to argue against the striking degree of success which characterizes the record of this government is difficult indeed. It seems to me that our hon. friends are driven to the last extremity in order to find some excuse for attacking the administration, and consequently they hope by this proposition to draw the wool over our eyes. Take my hon. friend from Parkdale (Mr. Spence), a man of commanding ability. I told him last evening that he had made a splendid sipeech except for the inherent weakness of his case; he was on the wrong side. He reminds me of that notable character recorded in the "Brownies of Bodesbec" under the name of Wattie Laid-law. Wattie was a real character in those Scottish days. He was always in trouble and continually appealing to his friends, one of whom would reply, "Aye, Wattie, if ye be in the right I'll help ye." This answer not appealing to his good judgment, he became quite irritated, and stamping his foot on the floor said, "She he's aye talkin' about being in the right, and in the right, but it's no when a man's in the right that he is in need of a frien', but when he is in the wrang." Then my hon. friend from Muskoka (Mr. Mc-Gibbon) seconds the attack of his colleague from Parkdale. They are both very much concerned about the farmer's welfare. They
7S594-221
remind me of the following rhyme which 1
once read in a New York journal:
"Stick to the farm", said the president To the wide-eyed country boy;
Then he hies him away to his White House home
With an air of rustic joy.
"Stick to the farm", said the railroad king To the lad who looked afar;
Then he hies him away on the double quick In his rustic private car.
"Stick to the farm", said the clergyman To the lad on the worm fence perched;
Then he lays his ear to the ground to hear A call to the city church.
"Stick to the farm," said the doctor wise To those who would break the rut;
Then he hies him away where appendix grows In bountiful crops to cut.

Whereas it is the existent policy of this country that reasonable -preparation should be made for self-defence in case of any war involving Canada;
And whereas, such preparation, if undertaken, should be both adequate and of such a character as to make war less likely to occur;

Si. Lawrence Waterway-Mr. Church
Therefore be it resolved that, in the opinion of this house, it is advisable that legislation be enacted providing that on the declaration of war involving Canada, all natural resources and essential services, including transportation facilities, industrial plants, and commercial and financial agencies, should automatically be conscripted for national service for the duration of the war.
He said: I would like this motion to stand for to-night, but I would like it to retain its position on the order paper.
